# The 15 source files in data/ are CRLF, and the entire bronze layer is built on
# that fact — 05_validate_bronze.sql asserts zero carriage returns survive
# ingestion, and data/CHECKSUMS.sha256 pins the exact bytes.
#
# Without this, byte-identity depends on each contributor's git config rather
# than on the repository. A contributor on Git for Windows defaults
# (core.autocrlf=true) who merely renames or re-adds a file would commit an
# LF-normalized blob: the checksum gate fails with what looks like a whole-file
# rewrite, and "fixing" it by regenerating the manifest would quietly make the
# CR-stripping logic and the no_carriage_returns checks vacuous.
data/** -text

# The manifest is generated, not delivered — it is LF and must stay LF. Left
# under `-text` above, regenerating it on a machine that emits CRLF (PowerShell
# redirection, a Windows editor) would append \r to all 15 paths and `shasum -c`
# would report every source file as "FAILED open or read" — a failure that reads
# as total corruption when nothing is actually wrong.
data/CHECKSUMS.sha256 text eol=lf

# Everything else is normal text and may be normalized.
*.sql   text
*.md    text
*.yml   text
*.yaml  text
